<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Count! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Hold Up Ten Fingers!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Count!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Show comprehension of basic commands used in the classroom.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can show comprehension of instructions in simple counting games (e.g., 'Hold up five fingers.').<br>Can repeat counting songs or rhymes (e.g., 'One, two, three, four, five.').<br>Can recognize spoken numbers up to ten when accompanied by gestures (e.g., hears 'three' and holds up three fingers).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the theme through engaging activities.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads the "One Little Finger" song with corresponding TPR actions. Students sing along and point to their body parts as indicated. This introduces 'finger' and 'hand' and reinforces numbers 1-5 through movement and song.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models how to count fingers, showing one finger, then two, then three, etc., up to ten. The teacher clearly pronounces the numbers and emphasizes the initial /k/ sound in 'count' and the /f/ sound in 'fingers' while demonstrating. The teacher holds up their hand and says "one finger," then "two fingers," etc., using gestures and clear articulation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions to check understanding.<br>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up three fingers) "Is this one finger?" (Students respond: "No!")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up five fingers) "Are these five fingers?" (Students respond: "Yes!")</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!". Focus on listening to the target language.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large picture flashcards depicting numbers 1-10 with corresponding quantities of fingers, blocks, or pencils. Audio recording of numbers 1-10 spoken clearly. Realia: blocks, pencils, books, crayons.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen for and understand spoken numbers 1-10 and basic commands related to counting and showing quantities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher displays a flashcard with five blocks. "How many blocks can you see?" (Students point or say the number). "What number is this?" (pointing to the numeral 5).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (TPR):</b> The teacher says a number (e.g., "three") and shows a flashcard of a different quantity (e.g., four pencils). Students give a "thumbs up" for true or "thumbs down" for false. The teacher can also ask, "Is this number five?" while showing the flashcard for 'seven'.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher says a number (e.g., "Number eight!"). Students listen and point to the corresponding number flashcard displayed around the classroom. The teacher repeats this for several numbers.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Guided activities to reinforce comprehension and language use.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for and responding to commands involving numbers 1-10 and classroom objects. Grammar: Quantity + nouns (e.g., 'ten fingers', 'three pencils'). Vocabulary: numbers (1-10), hand, finger, block, pencil, book, crayon. Pronunciation: Recognizing number sounds (1-10), initial /k/ and /f/.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Show (Controlled practice):</b> The teacher gives commands, and students follow by showing the correct number of fingers or pointing to objects. E.g., "Hold up two fingers." "Point to four blocks." "Show me one book." The teacher ensures all students are listening and responding correctly, providing individual feedback.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Count (Semi-controlled drill):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with a specific number of objects (e.g., three red crayons, five blue blocks) and asks, "How many red crayons can you see?" Students listen to the question and respond by counting aloud, e.g., "One, two, three red crayons." This also practices adjectives (red, blue).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Match (Group/Peer Check):</b> Students are in small groups with sets of numbers (1-10) written on individual cards and a pile of small objects (e.g., blocks, crayons). The teacher calls out a number (e.g., "Match six crayons!"). Students listen and then work together to place six crayons next to the number '6' card. The teacher circulates, observing and repeating commands for clarity, ensuring active listening and turn-taking.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(15-18 min)</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!". Focus on fluency and task completion.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Simon Says: Count!" - A TPR-based game where students demonstrate their listening comprehension by performing actions. The teacher leads the game, emphasizing clear listening before acting.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher explains the game: "If I say 'Simon Says,' you do it. If I don't say 'Simon Says,' don't do it!" The teacher gives various commands using target grammar and vocabulary.
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Simon Says, hold up five fingers!"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Simon Says, point to three books!"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Hold up eight fingers!" (Students who move are out, or teacher just reinforces the rule for Kinder).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Simon Says, show me one crayon!"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Simon Says, count ten fingers!"</li>
            </ul>
            The game encourages careful listening and quick, accurate responses. The teacher models the actions and praises correct responses, creating a fun, low-stress environment.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(8-10 min)</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection to check learning outcomes.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes individual students or small groups during the assessment task, noting their ability to follow spoken instructions and recognize numbers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Do." The teacher calls students individually or in very small groups. The teacher gives a series of 3-4 simple commands without the "Simon Says" prompt. E.g., "Hold up seven fingers." "Point to two pencils." "Show me four blocks." "Count to ten."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly follows commands involving numbers 1-10.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of target vocabulary (numbers, hand, finger, block, pencil, book, crayon).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows active listening by responding appropriately and promptly.</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks, "Can you hear number five?") Students give a "thumbs up" if they can, "thumbs down" if it's still difficult.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks, "Was it fun to count?") Students give a "happy face" or "sad face" gesture.</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement and connection to future lessons.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher guides students in a brief reflection.
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What numbers did we hear and count today?" (Students can shout out numbers they remember or hold up fingers).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite game with the numbers?" (Students can point to a picture or gesture).</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., TPR, the counting song, specific games).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with listening to numbers or commands? (e.g., distinguishing certain numbers, following multi-step commands).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled? (e.g., more visual aids, slower pace, more repetition).</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "Great counting today, everyone! Next time, we will not only hear the numbers, but we will also *see* the numbers written down in our books and on our papers! Get ready to read and find the numbers!"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(2-3 min)</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>